  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...Dry conditions will return by Monday and last  \nthroughout the week. Patchy frost may occur during Tuesday and \nWednesday mornings. \n\nAn upper level disturbance and cold front has moved southeast \nand out of the area. At the surface, high pressure centered \nover the Upper Midwest will gradually shift east. Another cold \nfront and the leading edge of higher pressure will push into the\nsouthern Appalachians overnight tonight into Monday morning, \nhelping to break down a CAD regime currently in place along the \nBlue Ridge. Following this, cloud cover should begin to erode, \nbut it looks like it may stick around most of the overnight into\ntomorrow morning. The clouds push south of the area/erodes \nmidday Monday into Monday afternoon. Any fog that forms \novernight now looks like it will be much more patchy and less \nlikely to be dense.\n\nMuch drier air arrives along with the high pressure. Low \ntemperatures for Tuesday and Wednesday mornings may support some\npatchy frost in the mountain valleys where locations such as \nthe Greenbrier River Valley and Burkes Garden may see lows in \n30s. The models maintain high pressure over the region for the \nremainder of the week. As the airmass modifies, temperatures \nshould moderate for the second half of the week.\n\nOur next opportunity for rain may occur next weekend into early\nnext week (as in 7-8 days from now). NHC is monitoring a broad \narea of low pressure in the Gulf, and this feature may develop \ninto a cyclone over the next week. It is possible the system\nmoves ashore and eventually brings some moisture to our area. \nThat is a lot of ifs, so for now have tempered high NBM PoPs \nwith some drier probabilistic guidance to keep rain chances \nbelow 50%.",
